From: Gran Turismo 6 for Mac <https://github.com/Wooloomooloo2/Gran-Turismo-6-Mac>
Subject: [PATCH] GCM: prepare-flip command in the title's context, present at
 the immediate flip; park holes wait; present-rect pitch

Upstream: not yet submitted; written to be upstreamed (generic)
Licence: MIT, as ps3recomp (see its LICENSE); offered under the same terms
Base: ps3recomp 4e8f3ca87a3e1d87bb83b8b7e9348fe2685cd514 (after 0001-0050)

What:
(1) cellGcmSetPrepareFlip / cellGcmSetFlipImmediate as libgcm and the RSX
do them. 0048 wrote a private 1-word marker only when the context had 4 KB
to spare past `current` (the runtime ring's reserve), so a context the title
owns and steps in small segments almost never had room: the prepare then
presented at call time and the immediate flip presented again, about two
presents per flip, every other one mid-frame (GT6: the 180-draw scene stage,
black). Now the prepare writes the driver's 2-word flip command (method
0xFEAC, data: the buffer id) at `current`; a title-owned context needs only
those 8 bytes, and with less the title's own callback is called first
(ctx->callback(ctx, 2)), as libgcm does. The runtime's own ring keeps its
reserve. The drain marks the buffer ready where it meets the command (and
resets the 3D state, as at a flip); cellGcmSetFlipImmediate presents a ready
buffer at once, or the drain presents it when it reaches the command: one
present per prepare + immediate pair, never an unfinished frame. A context
that is not a mapped command buffer marks the buffer ready at once; nothing
presents at the prepare any more. Per-buffer state is atomic (the prepare
runs on the title's render thread, the immediate flip from its vblank
handler). A resync that skips a prepare command marks its buffer ready.
Counters on stderr ("[gcm-prepare]", at prepares 1, 10, 100, 1000 and every
2000th; every one with GCM_PREPARE_STATS) give the path each prepare took and
how many the old 4 KB check would have refused.
(2) A self-JUMP park with commands already written after it is a hole an SPU
job fills, not a segment end; the RSX waits on it however long that takes.
The 50 ms grace expired on such holes under host load and the resync dropped
the block's tail, with the sync write the title then waited on for good.
Holes get GCM_PARK_HOLE_GRACE_MS (default 5000; 0 waits for ever, with a
line every 5 s); and a jump-park resync now sweeps the abandoned block's
remainder when it lies past put, outside the ring range the resync
sweeps (fences, semaphore and back-end releases, prepare commands).
(3) Present: a surface of another pitch than the display buffer's is not
cropped (the video out would scan that memory at the buffer's pitch); the
last presented rectangle is shown again (present_rect with surface 0). A
target bound with pitch 0 is logged (4 times), and one of exactly the
buffer's size is taken as laid out like it.
(4) 0050's trace comment says 4000, as its cap is.
Why: GT6 (BCES01977): flips presented about 1.9 times per prepare, the 26
black dumps of the verdict run were the mid-frame presents; a long run under
load stopped after two jump-park resyncs (20261029-034740).
Behaviour reference: RPCS3's cellGcmSetPrepareFlip and its GCM_FLIP_COMMAND
(0xFEAC) handling, read as a reference only, no code copied.
Tests: libs/video/tests/test_gcm_prepare_flip.c (a title-owned context with
under 4 KB of headroom takes the stream; the callback makes room; one present
per pair in either order), test_rsx_draw_engine.c (pitch mismatch keeps the
last image), test_gcm_resync_labels.c (a hole outlasts the segment-end grace,
and its block's tail release still lands).

